=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The Linux `iptables` FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three blanket DROP rules added for inbound traffic on eth2 (the 10.50.1.0/24 transit link to router3).

**Mechanism:** Location B hosts (PC15–18 on 172.18.51.0/24; Laptop4/5 on 172.18.50.0/24) have a correctly configured default gateway pointing to router6 (eth1: 172.18.51.254 and eth0: 172.18.50.254). All routing tables across the network are intact — router6 has RIP-learned routes to Location A (172.16.10.0/24) and Location C (192.168.200.0/24), and return kernel routes for the 172.18.50–51.0/24 subnets exist on router3, router2, router1, and router0. The outbound direction (B→A/C via eth2) is unimpeded. However, when Location A or C responds, the reply packets arrive back on router6's eth2 and hit the injected iptables rules: rule 2 drops all TCP (proto 6), rule 3 drops all UDP (proto 17), and rule 4 drops all ICMP (proto 0) — all matching inbound on eth2. Live proof: rule 4 had exactly 3 packets / 252 bytes caught, matching the 3 ICMP echo replies from the diagnostic ping I sent to router3 (84 bytes each = standard ICMP reply). No return traffic can reach Location B, making every destination outside of B appear unreachable from B's hosts.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, rules 2–4 (iptables inbound drop on eth2).

**Root cause:** Three DROP rules (`-p tcp/udp/0 -i eth2 -j DROP`) were injected into router6's iptables FORWARD chain, blocking all protocol return traffic from the WAN-facing interface.

**Corrective fix:** Delete the three DROP rules:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -p tcp -i eth2 -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -p udp -i eth2 -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -p icmp -i eth2 -j DROP
```
=== END ===